## Part 1 — first deploy, start to finish

### Step 0 — install two things

| Tool | Why | Install |
| ---- | --- | ------- |
| **Docker** | builds the application images; also runs terraform for you | [docs.docker.com](https://docs.docker.com/get-docker/) |
| **Azure CLI** (`az`) | signing in needs a browser, which a container doesn't have | `brew install azure-cli` · [other platforms](https://learn.microsoft.com/cli/azure/install-azure-cli) |

You do **not** need terraform on your machine.

### Step 1 — have the right access in Azure

An Azure subscription where your account can **create resource groups**
(*Contributor*) and **assign roles** (*User Access Administrator*). *Owner*
covers both. The role assignment matters because the container apps are granted
`AcrPull` on the registry — that is what lets them pull images without storing a
registry password on each app.

On a brand-new subscription, register the resource providers this scaffold uses
(free, once per subscription, a couple of minutes to propagate):

```bash
for p in App OperationalInsights Network Storage ContainerRegistry \
         DBforPostgreSQL ManagedIdentity Authorization; do
  az provider register --namespace Microsoft.$p
done
```

> Container Apps needs **no VM quota**, which is what makes this target work on
> restricted subscriptions where a Kubernetes cluster cannot be created at all.

### Step 2 — sign in for this environment

```bash
dude iac login --env dev
```

Runs `az login` if needed, then prints the subscription `dev` will deploy into
and the id to pin it with. Pin it — an unpinned environment silently follows
whatever `az account set` was run last:

```hcl
# iac/terraform/environments/dev/terraform.tfvars
subscription_id = "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000"
```

### Step 3 — create the shared resources (once per project)

```bash
dude iac bootstrap --state-prefix <yourorg> --location northeurope --env dev --yes
```

Creates, in `{{projectName}}-shared-rg`: a **storage account** + `tfstate`
container holding the Terraform state for every environment, and an **Azure
Container Registry** holding the images for every environment. Then it writes
their names into `environments/dev/{backend.hcl,terraform.tfvars}` for you.

`--state-prefix` makes those names globally unique across Azure; they allow only
lowercase letters and digits. Run this **once per project**, not per environment.

### Step 4 — initialise Terraform

```bash
dude iac init --env dev
```

### Step 5 — build, push and provision, in one step

```bash
dude iac ship --env dev
```

**Not `apply` first.** Container Apps validates that an image exists when it
creates an app — unlike Kubernetes, which accepts the app and leaves it in
ImagePullBackOff — so applying before anything is published fails outright.
`ship` builds both images, pushes them, records the tag and *then* applies, which
is the order that works on a first deploy. `dude iac apply` checks this for you
and says so rather than letting Azure produce an error that never mentions the
real problem.

Use `dude iac plan --env dev` to review the infrastructure first if you like; it
does not need the images.

Expect a few minutes.{{#if withPostgres}} The PostgreSQL Flexible Server is most
of it.{{/if}} What you get: a resource group, a VNet, a Log Analytics workspace,
a Container Apps environment, and one container app per service — frontend,
backend{{#if withCelery}}, Celery worker{{/if}}{{#if withCeleryBeat}}, Celery
beat{{/if}}{{#if withRedis}}, Redis{{/if}}{{#if withPostgres}} — plus a private
PostgreSQL Flexible Server, a migration job, and the migrations themselves run
and waited for{{/if}}.

After the first deploy, the two halves separate: `dude iac apply` for
infrastructure changes, `dude iac ship` (or `deploy`) to release new code.

### Step 6 — open it

```bash
dude iac output --env dev        # look for app_url
dude iac status --env dev        # every app, its state and revision
```

```
https://{{projectName}}-dev-frontend.<random>.northeurope.azurecontainerapps.io/
```

**HTTPS, with a certificate Microsoft issues and renews** — no domain to buy, no
DNS to delegate, no certificate authority to configure. The API is served under
`/api`, proxied by the frontend to the backend over the environment's internal
network.

If something is not up:

```bash
dude iac logs --env dev --service backend --follow
```

---

## How the pieces fit

| Component | Exposure | Notes |
| --------- | -------- | ----- |
| `frontend` | **public HTTPS** | nginx: serves the SPA and proxies `/api` to the backend |
| `backend` | internal only | reachable from inside the environment, never from the internet |
{{#if withCelery}}| `worker` | none | pulls from the broker; nothing connects to it |
{{/if}}{{#if withCeleryBeat}}| `beat` | none | exactly one replica — a second would fire every periodic task twice |
{{/if}}{{#if withRedis}}| `redis` | internal TCP | Celery broker + result backend |
{{/if}}{{#if withPostgres}}| PostgreSQL | private (VNet) | no public endpoint; `psql` from your laptop will not connect |
| migration job | n/a | manually triggered, runs `alembic upgrade head` |
{{/if}}

Apps in one Container Apps environment resolve each other **by app name**, which
is why the frontend proxies to `http://{{projectName}}-<env>-backend` with no
FQDN or service-discovery wiring.

### Why the frontend proxies instead of the SPA calling the API directly

There is no shared ingress doing path-based routing here — each app gets its own
hostname. Exposing the backend publicly too would mean a second URL baked into
the frontend bundle at build time, plus CORS. Proxying keeps one origin, so the
browser sees a single site and the backend stays off the internet.

## Image tags, and why they are committed

`dude iac deploy`/`ship` writes `image_tag` into the environment's
`terraform.tfvars` and applies. That makes the deployed version a **reviewed,
committed fact** rather than something imperative that the next `terraform
apply` would silently roll back.

| Working tree | Tag |
|--------------|-----|
| clean | `<git-short-sha>` |
| uncommitted changes | `<git-short-sha>-dirty-<hash-of-the-diff>` |

Commit the `image_tag` change after a release. Pushing a tag that already exists
**overwrites** it — a Basic-tier registry does not enforce tag immutability — so
`push`/`ship` warn before doing it.

## Cost, and scaling to zero

Container Apps bills per vCPU-second and GiB-second of *running* replicas. Set a
service's `min_replicas` to `0` and it costs nothing while idle, at the price of
a cold start on the next request. The defaults keep the frontend and backend at
1 — a visitor should not wait for a container to boot — while everything else is
sized down. In a dev environment you may well want `backend_min_replicas = 0`.

{{#if withPostgres}}The database is the opposite: a Flexible Server bills while it
exists, whether or not anything connects. Stop it from the portal, or destroy the
environment, when you are not using it.

{{/if}}## Add a new environment

```bash
dude iac new-env --env prod          # copies dev by default; --from <env> to pick another
```

Scaffolds `environments/prod/{backend.hcl,terraform.tfvars}` with
`environment = "prod"`, a per-env state `key`, and `image_tag` reset to `latest`
(a new environment must not come up serving whatever the source env last
deployed). The storage account, state container **and registry** are shared, so
you do not bootstrap again.

```bash
dude iac init --env prod && dude iac apply --env prod --yes && dude iac ship --env prod
```

## Local vs CI/CD

The same commands run in CI — state is remote and auth is the Azure CLI/SDK
chain. Authenticate a pipeline with a service principal or workload identity
federation by exporting `ARM_CLIENT_ID`, `ARM_TENANT_ID`, `ARM_SUBSCRIPTION_ID`
and either `ARM_CLIENT_SECRET` or OIDC. Pass an explicit `--tag $CI_COMMIT_SHA`
so the pipeline controls provenance.

## Tear down

```bash
dude iac destroy --env dev
```

Destroys the environment's whole resource group, re-checks that it is really
gone (a delete still in flight, or a resource lock, otherwise leaves it quietly
billing), then tears down the shared bootstrap — but **only if no other
environment still uses it**, judged from each sibling's remote Terraform state
rather than its folder on disk.

| Flag             | Effect                                                              |
| ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `--keep-backend` | Keep the shared state storage account **+ registry**.               |
| `--skip-tf`      | Skip the per-env destroy — only run the bootstrap teardown.         |

## Commands

| Command | Wraps |
|---------|-------|
| `dude iac login`     | `az login` (when needed) + `az account show` for the env's subscription |
| `dude iac new-env`   | scaffolds `environments/<name>/` from an existing env |
| `dude iac bootstrap` | `terraform init/apply` in `bootstrap/`, patches `backend.hcl` + `terraform.tfvars` |
| `dude iac init`      | `terraform init` (azurerm backend per env) |
| `dude iac plan`      | `terraform plan` |
| `dude iac apply`     | `terraform apply` (`--yes` to auto-approve) |
| `dude iac destroy`   | `terraform destroy` + bootstrap teardown (`--yes`, `--keep-backend`, `--skip-tf`) |
| `dude iac output`    | `terraform output` (`--json`) |
| `dude iac fmt`       | `terraform fmt -recursive` |
| `dude iac validate`  | `terraform validate` |
| `dude iac build`     | `docker build` BE+FE prod images (`linux/amd64`) |
| `dude iac push`      | registry token login + `docker push` BE+FE |
| `dude iac deploy`    | record `image_tag` + `terraform apply`{{#if withPostgres}} + run migrations{{/if}} |
| `dude iac ship`      | `build` + `push` + `deploy` |
| `dude iac status`    | every container app: state, revision, public URL |
| `dude iac logs`      | `az containerapp logs show` (`--service`, `--tail`, `--follow`) |
{{#if withPostgres}}| `dude iac migrate`   | starts the migration job and waits for it |
{{/if}}| `dude iac shell`     | interactive shell in the IaC runner container (terraform, az) |

All commands take `--env <name>` (**required**) and `--subscription <id>`.
`build`/`push`/`deploy`/`ship` take `--tag`; `build`/`ship` also `--platform`
(default `linux/amd64`). `bootstrap` accepts `--state-prefix` (required) and
`--location` (default `northeurope`).

## Troubleshooting

| Symptom | Cause / fix |
| ------- | ----------- |
| `apply` fails on `azurerm_role_assignment.acr_pull` with *AuthorizationFailed* | Your account can't assign roles. Ask for **User Access Administrator** (or Owner). |
| `apply` fails: *RequestDisallowedByAzure … region is currently not accepting new customers* | That region is capacity-closed to your subscription. Use another: `--location` **and** `location` in the env tfvars. |
| `apply` fails: subnet too small | The Container Apps subnet must be at least a `/23`. Widen `aca_subnet_cidr`. |
| `bootstrap` fails: storage account name taken | The name is global across Azure. Re-run with a different `--state-prefix`. |
| `init` fails: *ListBlobs … 404* | A brand-new storage account's blob endpoint takes a few minutes. `bootstrap` waits for it; if you hit this anyway, re-run `init` a minute later. |
| `apply` fails: *InvalidParameterValueInContainerTemplate … MANIFEST_UNKNOWN* | The configured `image_tag` is not in the registry. Container Apps refuses to create an app it cannot pull. Run `dude iac ship --env <env>`, which builds and pushes first. |
| Apps stuck, logs show image pull errors | The `AcrPull` role assignment may not have propagated yet (a few minutes). |
| `502` from the public URL | The backend is not up. `dude iac logs --env <env> --service backend`. |
{{#if withPostgres}}| `psql` from your laptop can't connect | By design: the database has no public endpoint and lives inside the VNet. |
{{/if}}| `destroy` removes the environment but leaves the shared resource group | The bootstrap's Terraform state is **local and gitignored**, so it exists only where `dude iac bootstrap` ran. A fresh clone or a re-scaffold starts empty and has nothing to destroy. Remove them directly: `az group delete --name <project>-shared-rg --yes`. Keep that state file (or the machine) if you want Terraform to own the teardown. |
| `exec format error` in an app | An arm64 image on x86 compute. Rebuild without overriding `--platform`. |
